1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,336
We Indians spend more than 33 percent of our income on EMIs.

2
00:00:04,336 --> 00:00:07,338
This is the result of a big study. Now think,

3
00:00:07,338 --> 00:00:10,673
if you lose your job tomorrow. Your salary stops,

4
00:00:10,673 --> 00:00:12,675
but the EMI date does not change.

5
00:00:12,675 --> 00:00:16,010
The bank doesn't care about the news of your job.

6
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:19,407
Listen to a friend's story, let's call him Karan.

7
00:00:19,407 --> 00:00:22,049
An IT job in Pune, a good salary,

8
00:00:22,049 --> 00:00:26,201
and a home loan for a flat. A car loan too,

9
00:00:26,201 --> 00:00:29,221
because the office was far. Every month the salary came,

10
00:00:29,221 --> 00:00:32,240
and a big part went straight into EMIs.

11
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:35,260
At home, his wife Neha and a small son.

12
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,819
Karan used to think, my work is good, the company is big.

13
00:00:38,819 --> 00:00:40,954
Why is there any need to save separately,

14
00:00:40,954 --> 00:00:43,445
there is a little in the savings account anyway.

15
00:00:43,445 --> 00:00:46,292
The money left over he put into shares,

16
00:00:46,292 --> 00:00:49,139
because returns looked higher there. Honestly,

17
00:00:49,139 --> 00:00:51,630
his logic didn't seem wrong.

18
00:00:51,630 --> 00:00:54,806
Then one Monday, a meeting was called with HR in the office.

19
00:00:54,806 --> 00:00:56,394
The company was cutting costs,

20
00:00:56,394 --> 00:00:58,935
and Karan's whole team was shut down.

21
00:00:58,935 --> 00:01:02,111
In his hand, the notice period salary and a cardboard box.

22
00:01:02,111 --> 00:01:04,017
Coming home he told Neha,

23
00:01:04,017 --> 00:01:06,240
and both sat silent for a long time.

24
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,827
The first month everything felt fine, because of the notice salary.

25
00:01:09,827 --> 00:01:11,978
In the second month the EMI date came,

26
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:14,489
and the account started emptying fast.

27
00:01:14,489 --> 00:01:18,434
In the third month Karan had to sell the shares, that too at a loss,

28
00:01:18,434 --> 00:01:22,379
because the market was down. The money that was meant for the long term,

29
00:01:22,379 --> 00:01:24,890
went into today's bills.

30
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:27,678
Right here is hidden the real lesson of the whole story.

31
00:01:27,678 --> 00:01:31,512
Karan had money, but not in the right place.

32
00:01:31,512 --> 00:01:33,604
He needed a separate pouch,

33
00:01:33,604 --> 00:01:36,043
only for a day like this.

34
00:01:36,043 --> 00:01:39,877
This is called an emergency fund, meaning money for hard times,

35
00:01:39,877 --> 00:01:41,620
that comes to your hand immediately.

36
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:45,813
So today, 3 questions. First, how much should an emergency fund be? Second,

37
00:01:45,813 --> 00:01:50,006
where to keep it, is keeping it in a savings account wrong? Third,

38
00:01:50,006 --> 00:01:53,818
should you pay off loans first or build the fund first? And finally,

39
00:01:53,818 --> 00:01:57,630
the mistakes that even people who earn well make.

40
00:01:57,630 --> 00:02:02,013
First question, how much should the emergency fund be? The answer isn't from salary,

41
00:02:02,013 --> 00:02:05,200
It starts with expenses. People think,

42
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,185
my salary is this much, so the fund should match it.

43
00:02:09,185 --> 00:02:13,169
This is the wrong start. If you lose your job, you don't need your salary,

44
00:02:13,169 --> 00:02:15,560
you need to cover your essential expenses.

45
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:17,161
Essential expenses means those

46
00:02:17,161 --> 00:02:21,563
that you have to pay no matter what. House rent,

47
00:02:21,563 --> 00:02:25,966
or home loan and other loan EMIs. Groceries, electricity, water,

48
00:02:25,966 --> 00:02:30,368
phone and gas. Children's school fees, insurance premiums and medicines.

49
00:02:30,368 --> 00:02:34,370
Restaurants, online shopping and vacations are not on this list.

50
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:38,177
Later, Neha wrote this same list in a notebook.

51
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:40,080
For the first time, both of them saw

52
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,126
how much it really costs to run the house.

53
00:02:43,126 --> 00:02:45,791
That number turned out much lower than the salary.

54
00:02:45,791 --> 00:02:49,979
And this number is the foundation of your emergency fund. Not the salary,

55
00:02:49,979 --> 00:02:50,740
essential expenses.

56
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:54,396
Now the question: how many months of fund? Financial planners say,

57
00:02:54,396 --> 00:02:58,051
those who have a home loan, or who have family depending on them,

58
00:02:58,051 --> 00:03:01,707
should keep at least 6 months of essential expenses.

59
00:03:01,707 --> 00:03:05,362
Dependents means the people who live on your income,

60
00:03:05,362 --> 00:03:07,190
like children or parents.

61
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:08,608
Why only 6 months?

62
00:03:08,608 --> 00:03:12,508
Because finding a new job takes time. Interview, offer, joining,

63
00:03:12,508 --> 00:03:15,699
then the first salary, all of it takes weeks.

64
00:03:15,699 --> 00:03:17,826
And in between, the EMI doesn't stop.

65
00:03:17,826 --> 00:03:22,080
This cushion also saves you from taking the wrong job out of panic.

66
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:23,385
Look at a simple calculation.

67
00:03:23,385 --> 00:03:26,322
If your essential expenses are 50,000 rupees a month,

68
00:03:26,322 --> 00:03:29,259
then a 6-month fund means 3 lakh rupees.

69
00:03:29,259 --> 00:03:32,522
This calculation is given in an ET report.

70
00:03:32,522 --> 00:03:33,827
The number looks big,

71
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:37,090
but this is 6 months of breathing room for your family.

72
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:39,798
Karan's father said on the phone, son,

73
00:03:39,798 --> 00:03:43,184
in our time everyone kept money in FDs, it's safe.

74
00:03:43,184 --> 00:03:46,908
There was some truth in what he said, the habit of saving is the most important.

75
00:03:46,908 --> 00:03:49,955
But the real question is, where is the money kept.

76
00:03:49,955 --> 00:03:53,340
And on the day of need, how quickly will it reach your hands.

77
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:56,720
So the first answer is simple. Make a list of essential expenses,

78
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:00,438
and multiply it by 6 months. That is your target.

79
00:04:00,438 --> 00:04:03,142
Now the second question, where there is the most confusion.

80
00:04:03,142 --> 00:04:06,184
Where should you keep this much money so that you get it immediately when needed,

81
00:04:06,184 --> 00:04:08,550
And not get eaten away by inflation?

82
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:12,083
Karan's first thought was, put everything in a savings account.

83
00:04:12,083 --> 00:04:15,616
Withdraw anytime with a card, send instantly through UPI.

84
00:04:15,616 --> 00:04:18,506
And honestly, in an emergency, speed matters the most.

85
00:04:18,506 --> 00:04:21,397
A hospital bill can come at night too,

86
00:04:21,397 --> 00:04:22,360
even on a Sunday.

87
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:26,176
But keeping the whole fund in a savings account has a cost.

88
00:04:26,176 --> 00:04:27,910
The interest there is low,

89
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:31,379
and every year inflation reduces the buying power of money.

90
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:35,195
Inflation means prices of things going up. The money looks safe,

91
00:04:35,195 --> 00:04:37,970
but inside it is wearing away.

92
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:40,560
The second problem is not about money, it's about people.

93
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:42,502
The money you see in front of you every day,

94
00:04:42,502 --> 00:04:46,062
gets spent. A sale comes, you buy a new phone,

95
00:04:46,062 --> 00:04:49,299
the fund goes down a bit. A friend's wedding comes, the fund goes down more.

96
00:04:49,299 --> 00:04:52,860
When everything is in one account, you don't even notice.

97
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:54,470
So what do planners say?

98
00:04:54,470 --> 00:04:57,691
Keep 30 to 40 percent of the fund in the bank,

99
00:04:57,691 --> 00:04:59,623
in a savings account or a sweep-in FD.

100
00:04:59,623 --> 00:05:03,165
This is the money you need the same day. A hospital deposit,

101
00:05:03,165 --> 00:05:04,132
a sudden trip,

102
00:05:04,132 --> 00:05:07,030
or a bill that can't wait till tomorrow.

103
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:08,540
What is a sweep-in FD?

104
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:10,955
It is an FD linked to your savings account.

105
00:05:10,955 --> 00:05:14,277
Money above a set limit automatically turns into an FD,

106
00:05:14,277 --> 00:05:16,089
and earns slightly more interest.

107
00:05:16,089 --> 00:05:17,900
When the account runs low on money,

108
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:20,920
the FD money comes back automatically.

109
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:23,447
And how safe is your money in the bank?

110
00:05:23,447 --> 00:05:25,614
DICGC is a company of the RBI,

111
00:05:25,614 --> 00:05:29,224
that provides insurance on bank deposits. In every bank,

112
00:05:29,224 --> 00:05:32,474
every depositor is insured up to 5 lakh rupees.

113
00:05:32,474 --> 00:05:35,001
This means even if something happens to the bank,

114
00:05:35,001 --> 00:05:37,890
you get your money back up to this limit.

115
00:05:37,890 --> 00:05:40,730
So be a little smart about it. If the fund is big,

116
00:05:40,730 --> 00:05:43,570
you can split it across different banks.

117
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:47,042
And keep the emergency fund in a separate account from your salary account.

118
00:05:47,042 --> 00:05:48,620
So that it sinks into your mind,

119
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:51,460
this money is not for everyday expenses.

120
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:54,923
Now the rest of the fund. Here a name comes up,

121
00:05:54,923 --> 00:05:58,039
That many people hear for the first time. Liquid fund.

122
00:05:58,039 --> 00:06:01,155
From the name, it sounds like some kind of water plan.

123
00:06:01,155 --> 00:06:05,310
In fact, it is the simplest and calmest form of a mutual fund.

124
00:06:05,310 --> 00:06:07,335
A liquid fund is a mutual fund

125
00:06:07,335 --> 00:06:10,710
that puts money into very short-term loans.

126
00:06:10,710 --> 00:06:14,423
Like lending to the government or big companies for a few weeks.

127
00:06:14,423 --> 00:06:16,785
It does not put money in the share market.

128
00:06:16,785 --> 00:06:20,160
That is why its value goes up and down very little.

129
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:21,189
Planners say,

130
00:06:21,189 --> 00:06:25,304
keep 60 to 70 percent of your emergency fund in a liquid fund.

131
00:06:25,304 --> 00:06:27,705
The reason is simple. Less risk than shares,

132
00:06:27,705 --> 00:06:30,792
and usually a better return than a savings account.

133
00:06:30,792 --> 00:06:35,250
When you sell, the money reaches your account by the next working day.

134
00:06:35,250 --> 00:06:37,852
And what if you need the money right now?

135
00:06:37,852 --> 00:06:40,455
For that, SEBI has a rule.

136
00:06:40,455 --> 00:06:43,801
SEBI is the regulator of the share market and mutual funds.

137
00:06:43,801 --> 00:06:47,890
A liquid fund offers instant redemption, meaning you can take out money immediately.

138
00:06:47,890 --> 00:06:51,980
Up to 50,000 rupees per day from each scheme, with some conditions.

139
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,300
So the whole plan looks something like this.

140
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:57,945
For the first day's expenses, use the bank, card and UPI instantly.

141
00:06:57,945 --> 00:07:01,259
If the need is bigger, withdraw instantly from the liquid fund.

142
00:07:01,259 --> 00:07:03,247
For the rest, place a redemption request,

143
00:07:03,247 --> 00:07:06,230
and it will arrive in your account on the next working day.

144
00:07:06,230 --> 00:07:09,780
Keep one thing clear. Low risk does not mean

145
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:13,330
there is no risk at all. When choosing a liquid fund, pick a big,

146
00:07:13,330 --> 00:07:16,170
well-known fund house and look at the direct plan.

147
00:07:16,170 --> 00:07:20,075
A direct plan means without an agent, and it has lower costs.

148
00:07:20,075 --> 00:07:22,560
Do not run after higher returns here.

149
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:24,629
Now think about Karan's story again.

150
00:07:24,629 --> 00:07:27,732
If he had had such a fund worth 6 months of expenses,

151
00:07:27,732 --> 00:07:31,524
he would not have had to sell his shares at a loss. The market was down,

152
00:07:31,524 --> 00:07:34,282
but he could have waited calmly.

153
00:07:34,282 --> 00:07:37,730
An emergency fund is really the bodyguard of your other investments.

154
00:07:37,730 --> 00:07:41,192
The third question, and this is the one asked the most.

155
00:07:41,192 --> 00:07:45,038
Should you repay the loan first, or build the fund first? The heart says,

156
00:07:45,038 --> 00:07:47,346
finish the loan and save the interest.

157
00:07:47,346 --> 00:07:49,654
This logic also sounds good.

158
00:07:49,654 --> 00:07:53,500
But Karan's story teaches one more thing here.

159
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:54,196
Suppose

160
00:07:54,196 --> 00:07:58,025
you put all your leftover money into prepaying your home loan.

161
00:07:58,025 --> 00:08:01,853
Prepayment means paying off part of your loan before it is due.

162
00:08:01,853 --> 00:08:04,985
Your loan gets smaller, which is good. But if you lose your job,

163
00:08:04,985 --> 00:08:07,422
the bank will not give that money back.

164
00:08:07,422 --> 00:08:09,510
The EMI will still come every month.

165
00:08:09,510 --> 00:08:11,596
So here is a smart way to go about it.

166
00:08:11,596 --> 00:08:14,029
First, build a small cushion of a few months.

167
00:08:14,029 --> 00:08:15,767
Then run both together,

168
00:08:15,767 --> 00:08:18,201
keep filling the fund and keep paying the EMI.

169
00:08:18,201 --> 00:08:20,634
When the fund reaches 6 months,

170
00:08:20,634 --> 00:08:22,720
then put the extra money into prepayment.

171
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:26,743
One more check is important: what is your total EMI?

172
00:08:26,743 --> 00:08:28,205
One study says,

173
00:08:28,205 --> 00:08:32,594
Indians spend more than 33 percent of their income on EMIs.

174
00:08:32,594 --> 00:08:34,056
Credit experts say,

175
00:08:34,056 --> 00:08:37,713
keep your total EMI within 30 percent of your take-home salary.

176
00:08:37,713 --> 00:08:41,370
Take-home means the money that reaches your hand after deductions.

177
00:08:41,370 --> 00:08:44,195
If your EMI is above 30 percent,

178
00:08:44,195 --> 00:08:48,079
then stop before taking a new loan. A new phone on EMI,

179
00:08:48,079 --> 00:08:49,492
a new bike on EMI,

180
00:08:49,492 --> 00:08:52,316
every small EMI eats up your cushion.

181
00:08:52,316 --> 00:08:55,494
Karan's car loan also came like this,

182
00:08:55,494 --> 00:08:57,260
just for a little comfort.

183
00:08:57,260 --> 00:09:01,094
Now, the mistakes that even people who earn well make.

184
00:09:01,094 --> 00:09:04,579
First mistake: putting your emergency fund in the share market.

185
00:09:04,579 --> 00:09:07,716
A brother gives a tip: this share will do really well.

186
00:09:07,716 --> 00:09:11,550
But an emergency fund is not meant to grow, it is meant to be there on time.

187
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:13,990
A falling market does not care about your emergency.

188
00:09:13,990 --> 00:09:16,052
Second mistake: breaking the fund for a holiday,

189
00:09:16,052 --> 00:09:19,350
a sale, or wedding shopping.

190
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:23,061
These all feel necessary, but they are not emergencies.

191
00:09:23,061 --> 00:09:27,597
An emergency means job loss, illness, an accident, or sudden repairs at home.

192
00:09:27,597 --> 00:09:31,720
For things that you can plan, keep a separate saving.

193
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:35,795
Third mistake: forgetting to include EMI when calculating the fund.

194
00:09:35,795 --> 00:09:39,870
Fourth mistake: not refilling the fund after using it.

195
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:43,945
A fund is built once, but you have to keep it alive.

196
00:09:43,945 --> 00:09:47,650
If your household expenses go up, increase the fund too.

197
00:09:47,650 --> 00:09:49,087
So what should you do?

198
00:09:49,087 --> 00:09:52,321
Tonight, make a list of your essential household expenses.

199
00:09:52,321 --> 00:09:55,555
Multiply it by 6 months, that is your target.

200
00:09:55,555 --> 00:09:56,633
On salary day,

201
00:09:56,633 --> 00:09:59,867
Let one fixed portion go automatically into the emergency account.

202
00:09:59,867 --> 00:10:03,820
This setting can be done in the bank app, so you don't have to think about it every day.

203
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:07,753
Split the fund between the bank and a liquid fund, like we saw.

204
00:10:07,753 --> 00:10:10,971
Also look at health insurance and term insurance,

205
00:10:10,971 --> 00:10:14,904
because one big bill can eat up the fund in one stroke.

206
00:10:14,904 --> 00:10:18,480
This is not investment advice. According to your own needs,

207
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:19,910
make your decision carefully.

208
00:10:19,910 --> 00:10:22,960
A few months later, Karan got a new job.

209
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:25,671
Now there is a separate account in his house,

210
00:10:25,671 --> 00:10:28,043
which Neha named Chhatri (Umbrella).

211
00:10:28,043 --> 00:10:29,738
Because rain doesn't come with a warning.

212
00:10:29,738 --> 00:10:32,788
But the umbrella has to be kept at home beforehand,

213
00:10:32,788 --> 00:10:35,160
that is the whole philosophy of an emergency fund.

214
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:37,470
In the next video, we'll talk about the thing

215
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:40,440
that empties an emergency fund the fastest.

216
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:43,080
The hospital bill. Those lines in health insurance

217
00:10:43,080 --> 00:10:46,380
that you understand only on the day of the claim. Before that,

218
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:48,690
I have a small question for you.

219
00:10:48,690 --> 00:10:51,786
How many months' worth is your emergency fund right now?

220
00:10:51,786 --> 00:10:55,270
Just write the number in the comments. No one will judge,

221
00:10:55,270 --> 00:10:59,528
this is for you. And if there is nothing at all right now,

222
00:10:59,528 --> 00:11:03,011
then start tonight with a list of your expenses.

223
00:11:03,011 --> 00:11:05,333
The first step is the most important.
